From: =?UTF-8?q?Geraldo=20J=C3=BAnior?=
 <19914760+geraldoantonio@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 09:21:26 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] unix: save SRAM and exit cleanly on SIGTERM
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The emulator had no signal handlers, so stopping it with SIGTERM (for
example when the system shuts down) lost the SRAM changes not yet
auto-saved. Set a flag in the handler and call S9xExit() from the
joypad read, which saves SRAM like the normal quit keys.

Upstream: not sent, the project is unmaintained.

Signed-off-by: Geraldo Júnior <19914760+geraldoantonio@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 unix/unix.cpp | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/unix/unix.cpp b/unix/unix.cpp
index f3b5bd7..4795a1f 100644
--- a/unix/unix.cpp
+++ b/unix/unix.cpp
@@ -255,6 +255,18 @@ static int get_integer_conf (char *section, char *option, int defval)
 }
 
 
+/*
+ * SIGTERM/SIGINT/SIGHUP: leave through S9xExit() on the next joypad read,
+ * so SRAM is saved (e.g. when the system is shutting down).
+ */
+static volatile sig_atomic_t quit_signal = 0;
+
+static void S9xQuitSignal (int sig)
+{
+	(void)sig;
+	quit_signal = 1;
+}
+
 /*#include "cheats.h"*/
 extern "C"
 int main (int argc, char **argv)
@@ -267,6 +279,11 @@ int main (int argc, char **argv)
 	setvbuf(stdout, NULL, _IOLBF, 0);
 	fprintf(stderr, "snes9x: starting\n");
 
+	/* installed before SDL_Init, so SDL keeps these handlers */
+	signal(SIGTERM, S9xQuitSignal);
+	signal(SIGINT, S9xQuitSignal);
+	signal(SIGHUP, S9xQuitSignal);
+
 	char abspath[PATH_MAX];
 
 	//Set the directory to where the binary is
@@ -1212,6 +1229,11 @@ uint32 S9xReadJoypad (int which1)
 		if (joy_buttons[which1][sfc_joy[DOWN_2]])	val |= SNES_DOWN_MASK;
 	}
 
+	if (quit_signal) {
+		fprintf(stderr, "snes9x: quit signal, saving SRAM\n");
+		S9xExit();
+	}
+
 	if (keyssnes[sfc_key[QUIT]] == SDL_PRESSED || joy_buttons[0][sfc_joy[QUIT]]) S9xExit();
 
 	if (which1==0 && (val & SNES_SELECT_MASK) && (val & SNES_START_MASK)) S9xExit();
